{
 "note": "R1-4: the ingredients for ONE Class A selection-function figure.\n\n\"For a transmitter of EIRP P, drift nudot, duty cycle f, at frequency nu, what\nfraction of the 60 Class A systems would this experiment have detected?\"\n\nRead-only on the released catalogue.  The only thing this script contributes is\nthe measured Class A multiplier from the round-7 campaign, applied the same way\nthe catalogue already applies the old one -- verified, not assumed: in\nper_target_results_v3.99.csv, eirp_p90_sel_W / eirp_nominal_W is EXACTLY 5.700\nin all 403 Class A rows and 4.554 in all 1,252 Class B rows, i.e. a flat\nper-class constant.  That flatness is itself R1-5's answer: every window's\nquoted sensitivity is the nominal trigger times a transferred constant.\n\nConditioning is stated, not compressed away (R1-4 is explicit that a single\nfigure of merit would be misleading):\n  * frequency -- a transmitter is only detectable if it radiates inside a\n    window this survey actually observed toward THAT system, so the union\n    bandwidth per system is reported alongside;\n  * drift -- recoverable only if |nudot| <= that window's drift ceiling;\n  * duty cycle -- the carrier must be on during that block's on-source time;\n    a duty cycle f multiplies the recoverable flux by 1/f in the limit where\n    the carrier is on for a contiguous fraction f of the dwell, so the EIRP\n    axis scales as 1/f and the curve is drawn for f = 1, 0.5, 0.1;\n  * linewidth -- unresolved, i.e. W <= the window's channel width.\n",
